// THIS MODULE HAS A CONSUMER, AND THAT IS NOT A FORMALITY. An unimported carrier is typechecked by no
// closure and asserted by nothing -- the orphan shape where a module is CITED as an authority while
// nothing compiles it. A roster of outstanding obligations is exactly the kind of file that rots that
// way, so it carries its own witness.
//
// The assertion is deliberately narrow: every row names a module, a symbol and a proposition, and the
// roster is not empty. It does NOT check that the named sites still alias -- nothing does, which is
// the gap declared at the top of this file. Emptying the roster to make an obligation disappear goes
// red here; migrating a site and deleting its row does not, which is the direction the contract is
// supposed to allow.
fn aliased_predicate_site_is_named(row: AliasedPredicateSite) -> Bool {
  row.module_path != "" && row.symbol != "" && row.proposition != "" && row.aliased_causes != ""
}

test fn every_roster_row_names_its_site() -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: class_b_remaining_sites,
    empty: true,
    cons: fn(ok, row) { ok && aliased_predicate_site_is_named(row: row) }
  )
}

test fn the_roster_is_not_empty_while_the_class_is_open() -> Bool {
  fold_list(xs: class_b_remaining_sites, empty: false, cons: fn(_acc, _row) { true })
}


// ================================================================================================
// THE NEGATIVE-CONTROL CENSUS, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT QUERY AND NOT A SUBSET OF THE ONE ABOVE.
//
// A search for PREDICATE CAUSE ALIASING and a search for EXPECTING-RED ARMS ASSERTING A BARE FALSE
// return different populations. An arm can be perfectly unaliased along its intended path and still
// have this defect, because the aliasing enters through the BENCH rather than through the predicate.
// The rows above came from the aliasing query; these came from a second, separate one, and the
// distinction is recorded because a reader who assumes one covers the other will under-search exactly
// the more dangerous half.
//
// WHY THE MORE DANGEROUS HALF. In a positive arm a broken bench makes the assertion FAIL: loud,
// visible, someone investigates. In a NEGATIVE arm the polarity inverts and a broken bench makes it
// SUCCEED. The defect is then not merely undetected, it is actively reported as coverage, and it stays
// silent for exactly as long as the bench stays broken. tools.namespace_import_closure_behavioral_
// transport was found this way -- its red arm asserted `red_arm == false`, which a failed mktemp, a
// failed fixture write, a failed out-dir mkdir, or a gunbc that never spawned all satisfied.
//
// THE GENERAL RULE, stated so the next author gets it right by construction rather than by finding
// this list: A NEGATIVE CONTROL MUST ASSERT THAT THE MECHANISM PRODUCED A VERDICT, AND SEPARATELY THAT
// THE VERDICT WAS THE REFUSAL. Never that some Bool is false. v2.std.subject_evidence supplies both
// halves -- subject_evidence_produced_a_verdict and a licensing predicate that declines -- and the
// carrier's own note says why the second is deliberately not the negation of the first.
//
// SCOPE OF THIS CENSUS, stated because an unqualified list reads as the class. It covers discovered
// `test fn` arms reaching a host effect whose assertion is a bare equality against false or a bare
// negation, with no companion verdict-production assertion. It does NOT cover the same shape inside
// ordinary transport functions -- four such sites are already carried as rows above -- and it does not
// cover an arm whose bare falsity is written some third way. Being a floor rather than a census is the
// same limit the list above carries and for the same reason: nothing checks it.
type NegativeControlSite {
  module_path: String
  symbol: String
}

